<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>Six items. You approved draft 12 at 11:53 UTC on 5 November, and commit <code>23f18f024</code> landed it at 11:54. A cold read of it, read as the coordinating session, found 12 flags. The coordinator settled them in seven decisions, and at 11:57 the skill writer's commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> fixed the text. Each item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the words that you approved on version 11 of the earlier page. The words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. Where version 11 did not show the text, the item says so, and the diff is against the text before. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l1" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 1 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, the second bullet</span>The report of a landed change, and its reversal</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, line 125</p>
<ul><li>After a change lands, have a worker update the page of skill drafts, whose link the boot note holds (<code>references/skill-rewrite.md</code>, “The method”, step 7). <span class="cut">Then</span> <span class="add">When the worker is done,</span> tell the curator in one line, in your next message, with the <span class="cut">page's link.</span> <span class="add">commit and the page's link. Watch the page for comments sent to Claude.</span> If the curator asks to reverse a change, <span class="cut">by a comment on its item or in chat,</span> have the skill writer restore the old words <span class="cut">at once,</span> <span class="add">when the curator asks, by a comment sent to Claude on the item or in chat,</span> with no cold read. <span class="add">The reversal does not wait for the end of the curator's reading.</span> A change that nobody reads can weaken how you and the curator work together, and nobody notices.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. Commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> then changed it in three places. “When the worker is done” and “the commit and the” answer flag F10: the line did not say what it names, or whether it waits for the worker; the coordinator decided that it names the commit and the page. “Watch the page for comments sent to Claude” answers flag F9, and is the writer's own: without it, nothing makes a reversal comment reach the session. The reversal “when the curator asks”, and its last sentence, answer flag F8: a reversal comment could be held until your reading ended; the coordinator decided that it does not wait.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l2" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 2 &middot; SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, the third bullet</span>A waiting change goes under “Proposed changes”</h3>
