<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, “The curator's role and decisions”, line 126</p>
<ul><li>A change that touches the curator's role and decisions (this section), the asks, the held list, the review list or the register in which you write to the curator (the bullets at the head of “Talking to the curator”) waits for the curator's yes to its exact words. So does every rewrite of <code>SKILL.md</code> or of a whole part, since it can touch any rule. Put it as an item under <span class="cut">the page's proposed changes.</span> <span class="add">“Proposed changes” on the page of skill drafts.</span> Such a change has no default: it stays out of the skill until the yes. If you are unsure whether a change touches these, it waits.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet landed at your approval of 11:53, in commit <code>23f18f024</code>. Commit <code>f1dff89b4</code> answers flag F11: “the page's” leaned on the bullet before it, so the sentence now names the page and its section.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l3" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 3 &middot; SKILL.md, “Pages”, a new bullet</span>The form of a landed item</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
